<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michel de Montaigne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1533">1533</a>–<a href="/years/1592">1592</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Michel Eyquem, Seigneur de Montaigne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michel de Montaigne was a writer of the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> who popularised the essay as a literary genre, born on his wealthy family's estate, the Château de Montaigne near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, and brought up by his father's plan with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as his first language. After giving up his magistracy in Bordeaux, he retired by 1571 to the tower of his château, where among some 1,500 volumes he wrote the Essais, first published in 1580, which merge anecdote and autobiography with reflection. Elected mayor of Bordeaux in 1581, he was admired in his lifetime more as a statesman than as an author, and he is best known for his sceptical remark What do I know? He died at his château in 1592.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michel-de-montaigne.jpg" alt="Michel de Montaigne" width="330" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Michel_de_Montaigne,_circa_unknown.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the late 16th century humanism had ceased to be a movement of innovators and had become the ordinary content of education and learning, practised by late humanists such as <b>Michel de Montaigne</b>, whose Essays first appeared in 1580, and Justus Lipsius.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I find that there is nothing barbarous and savage in this nation, by anything that I can gather, excepting, that every one gives the title of barbarism to everything that is not in use in his own country.&#8221;</p><footer>Essays, book 1, &quot;Of Cannibals&quot; &#183; tr. Charles Cotton, 1686, rev. W. Carew Hazlitt, 1877 &#183; of the peoples of Brazil, as travellers had described them to him &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Essays_of_Montaigne/Book_I/Chapter_XXX" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I conceive there is more barbarity in eating a man alive, than when he is dead…&#8221;</p><footer>Essays, book 1, &quot;Of Cannibals&quot; &#183; tr. Charles Cotton, 1686, rev. W. Carew Hazlitt, 1877 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Essays_of_Montaigne/Book_I/Chapter_XXX" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The premeditation of death is the premeditation of liberty; he who has learned to die has unlearned to serve.&#8221;</p><footer>Essays, book 1, &quot;That to study philosophy is to learn to die&quot; &#183; tr. Charles Cotton, 1686, rev. W. Carew Hazlitt, 1877 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Essays_of_Montaigne/Book_I/Chapter_XIX" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…we must reserve a backshop, wholly our own and entirely free, wherein to settle our true liberty, our principal solitude and retreat.&#8221;</p><footer>Essays, book 1, &quot;Of Solitude&quot; &#183; tr. Charles Cotton, 1686, rev. W. Carew Hazlitt, 1877 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Essays_of_Montaigne/Book_I/Chapter_XXXVIII" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
